# Editorial first difference: paused projection retains the prior placement

**Finding.** The final3 editorial pixel gate remains failed on both backends. Its first material scene difference is the body flow exclusion polygon, which is a stale projection from the animated-to-paused transition. The final camera, obstacle, and text world transforms agree across candidate and cold main, but the committed polygons do not agree with a projection of those final transforms. This is a benchmark workload state bug; the captures do not establish a Glyph engine regression.

## Evidence and mechanism

- The authenticated final3 comparisons report 32,170 differing WebGPU pixels and 36,762 differing WebGL2 pixels at exact zero tolerance (editorial-comparison-final3/{webgpu,webgl2}.json). Their diff bounds, [251,282,1053,553], cover the body. The candidate and main delivery receipts identify different installed archive SHA-256 values (06fe22b7… and bbb3375c…) and completed scene captures.
- In both scene proofs, configuration, camera, obstacle world matrix, authored text, constraints, and text world transforms are identical across archives. The body keeps 1,443 inspected glyphs and 48 lines. No line text spans, glyph spans, or break counts change; 17 body line advances change numerically. The left polygon's first x coordinate is 312.179260 vs 312.273865 on WebGPU, and 312.196167 vs 311.840698 on WebGL2. The flow differences propagate to 1,064 body glyph x rows. Stable IDs and font handles differ by archive lifecycle and are not glyph geometry.
- I independently called the current projectTextFlowBounds producer using the **captured final** camera matrix/projection, body text world matrix, obstacle world matrix/local bounds, and each region's recorded clipping bounds. The calculated first left vertex is [314.5678405761719, 99.56187438964844] in all four cases. Maximum stored-versus-reprojected vertex displacement is 2.389 / 2.294 layout pixels for WebGPU candidate/main and 2.372 / 2.727 for WebGL2 candidate/main in the left region; the right region reaches 3.241 / 3.113 and 3.218 / 3.701 respectively. Thus the stored flow is inconsistent with the captured final scene, while a final-state projection converges across archives and backends. This in-memory check read the final scene JSON and the local projection source; it made no checkout edits or browser captures.
- The probe explicitly starts editorial animation, then pauseEditorialForCapture turns it off and waits for two completed paused frames (benches/scripts/run-presentation-workload-probe.mts, around lines 875–903 and 600–637). In animateEditorialEntries (benches/src/workloads/editorial/scene.ts, around lines 254–294), the first paused call sets the obstacle to timestamp zero and projects onto the body's **current** text plane before changing width/flow and calling layoutEditorialEntries. That layout can move the text plane to the final position. A later paused frame computes a corrected projection but returns before publishing it whenever editorialProjectionInitialized is true and width differs from lastWidth by less than one pixel. The gate observes the new obstacle and text transforms alongside the old flow. The initial animated width/placement was not captured, so its precise values and the exact first differing frame remain unobserved; the captured state and source ordering establish the stale-flow mechanism.

The lede is a separate, small residual: the inspected lede has the same four line rows, 257 glyphs, and 57 x/inkX rows differing by at most 0.00006103515625 layout pixels. The cold-main aggregate measure().glyphCount serialization of zero versus candidate 257 was known before this wave; the inspected glyph count is 257 on both. Neither detail explains the body polygon discrepancy, and neither warrants normalizing glyphs or widening pixel tolerance.

## Smallest source correction and oracle

Replace the paused width-only return with a condition that checks the **committed projected flow** as well as the exact target width. Since the routine already computes flow on every paused frame, compare its known two regions, bounds, and optional polygon exclusions against body.text.flow using exact numeric/structural equality. Publish and lay out whenever the flow differs, even if width is unchanged. A second paused frame then projects from the text plane produced by the first paused layout and commits that corrected flow; stable later frames can return without republishing. A source implementation should keep this comparison local to the editorial workload and avoid a generic serializer or arbitrary tolerance. A camera, obstacle, or text transform change while paused must similarly cause an updated flow to publish. The width check should be exact for this fixed-state path because a subpixel exact constraint can change a polygon.

The deterministic oracle is the captured final-state reprojection above: after an animated-to-paused transition, the committed body.text.flow must equal projectTextFlowBounds evaluated from the same final matrices for both regions, and a further paused frame must leave it unchanged without another reflow. Exercise that state transition through the real workload/host path, then rerun the authenticated candidate/main scene-only captures and strict zero-tolerance comparison on both WebGPU and WebGL2. A successful source fix has not yet been built or captured here; the strict pixel gate remains failed, and the independent lede float residual still needs evaluation after the body flow is corrected.
